        <section id="course" class="card" aria-labelledby="course-title">
          <h2 id="course-title">Course Description &amp; Structure</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Description:</strong> Emphasizes argumentative writing and continued instruction in drafting and revising
            essays in Standard English. Includes critical reading, logical thinking, responding to texts, addressing audiences,
            and researching/documenting credible academic sources. Credit is not available by special examination.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Instructor’s role:</strong> Provides detailed instruction and resources for analyzing texts (various modes) and
            facilitates reading discussions.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="rsi" class="card" aria-labelledby="rsi-title">
          <h2 id="rsi-title">Regular &amp; Substantive Interaction</h2>
          <p>
            Distance education requires meaningful interaction between students and instructors.
            Substantive interaction includes teaching, assessing, responding to questions, facilitating discussions, or other approved activities.
            Regular interaction is frequent/predictable with timely, substantial feedback and improvement guidance.
          </p>

          <details>
            <summary>How this course meets RSI <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <ul>
              <li>Announcements at the start and end of each week with reminders and commentary on class progress/work.</li>
              <li>Answer keys for quizzes/homework shared the following week.</li>
              <li>Opportunities to meet via Blackboard Collaborate before each major essay/project.</li>
              <li>Detailed feedback on essays and homework, plus instructor engagement in weekly discussion boards.</li>
              <li>Essay Group Q&amp;A sessions held the week of (or before) a project due date; times shared via announcements.</li>
            </ul>
          </details>
        </section>

        <section id="prereq" class="card" aria-labelledby="prereq-title">
          <h2 id="prereq-title">Prerequisites &amp; Required Materials</h2>

          <h3>Prerequisite</h3>
          <p>ENG 101</p>

          <h3>Texts &amp; Materials</h3>
          <p>
            This course is fully <strong>OER</strong> (Open Educational Resources). There is no required textbook or course charge model.
            Required readings will be linked or attached in Blackboard and are still required.
          </p>

          <div class="callout" role="note" aria-label="Due date and weekly folder note">
            <strong>Weekly workflow:</strong>
            Weekly folders open the Wednesday before the week starts (or after completing a “Grade Check” post).
            Unless otherwise noted, materials are due <strong>Sunday at 11:59 PM CST</strong>.
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="competencies" class="card" aria-labelledby="competencies-title">
          <h2 id="competencies-title">Course Competencies</h2>
          <p>Upon completion of this course, the student can:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Research topics to support an effective argument.</li>
            <li>Write with a fluent, versatile prose style.</li>
            <li>Analyze critically an assigned text.</li>
            <li>Document sources in formal academic writing without plagiarism.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="ethics" class="card" aria-labelledby="ethics-title">
          <h2 id="ethics-title">Online Workplace Ethics &amp; Participation</h2>

          <p>
            SKYCTC incorporates workplace ethics expectations that support student success and employability.
            Success is also measured by being present and punctual, participating in academic activities, and maintaining professional behavior.
          </p>

          <details>
            <summary>Present, punctual, and participating <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <ul>
              <li>Online participation includes more than logging in—students must complete learning activities and demonstrate progress.</li>
              <li>Students who do not participate or complete coursework according to guidelines may be withdrawn.</li>
              <li>Withdrawals for inactivity prior to the midpoint generally result in a <strong>W</strong>; after midpoint, a failing grade with an <strong>E</strong> may be issued.</li>
            </ul>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Professional conduct <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <ul>
              <li>Engage instructors, employees, and peers with respect.</li>
              <li>Use appropriate online etiquette.</li>
              <li>Utilize technology appropriately.</li>
              <li>Model behavior appropriate to your chosen profession.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="muted">
              Disruptive conduct may result in dismissal from the course and further disciplinary action.
            </p>
          </details>

          <p><strong>Proctored exam:</strong> This course does <strong>not</strong> require a proctored exam.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="policies" class="card" aria-labelledby="policies-title">
          <h2 id="policies-title">Key Policies</h2>

          <details>
            <summary>Withdrawal policy <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <p>
              Through the midterm of the session, students may officially withdraw and receive a <strong>W</strong>.
              After midterm, a student may request to withdraw and receive a W at the discretion of the instructor.
              Students must initiate the official withdrawal; withdrawal may impact financial aid and academic progress.
              The last day to withdraw is listed on the academic calendar; requests are made via self-service.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Financial aid statement <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <p>
              Title IV recipients (PELL, SEOG, student loans) who stop attending or withdraw prior to the 60% point may be obligated
              to repay a portion of financial aid.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Inquiries &amp; class concerns <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <p>
              Start by speaking with the instructor. If the issue is not resolved, contact the Dean of Arts &amp; Humanities,
              Dr. Stephanie Tolliver-Hyman (<a href="mailto:stephanie.hyman@kctcs.edu">stephanie.hyman@kctcs.edu</a>).
              To schedule an appointment, contact the administrative assistant at <a href="tel:+12709011051">270-901-1051</a>.
              If needed, refer to the formal academic appeal process in the KCTCS Code of Student Conduct.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Freedom from retaliation:</strong> Faculty and staff shall not retaliate against students for bringing concerns or complaints.</p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Student discrimination &amp; grievance procedures <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <p>
              KCTCS has zero tolerance for illegal discrimination. Students may pursue informal and/or formal grievance procedures.
              Contact the Director of Student Life and Engagement within 30 calendar days of the alleged incident.
              Informal resolution efforts occur within specified timelines; a formal complaint may be filed if not resolved.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Contact:</strong> Dr. David Travis • <a href="tel:+12709014348">270-901-4348</a> •
              <a href="mailto:david.travis@kctcs.edu">david.travis@kctcs.edu</a><br />
              1845 Loop Drive, Bowling Green, KY 42101
            </p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Plagiarism, cheating, and AI use <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <p>
              KCTCS has a policy on plagiarism and cheating (Code of Student Conduct, Article II, Standard 1).
            </p>
            <div class="callout" role="note" aria-label="AI policy note">
              <strong>AI policy:</strong> Use of AI in this course is strictly prohibited except non-generative AI embedded in
              KCTCS-approved technology (e.g., Microsoft Word, Grammarly, Blackboard). Using AI for brainstorming, composing responses,
              or significant contribution to assignments is forbidden.
            </div>
            <p>
              Assignments showing characteristics of unethical AI use will receive a zero until the student meets with the instructor.
              First offense: zero (no redo/resubmit). Second offense: course grade of “E.”
            </p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Privacy and security (FERPA) <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <p>
              The Registrar’s Office follows FERPA guidelines to protect student records. KCTCS colleges maintain confidentiality of
              educational records and accord applicable rights under FERPA, regardless of course delivery mode.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details>
            <summary>Blackboard time zone <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <p>
              Blackboard Ultra uses the student’s current geographic time zone. Due dates appear in that time zone (Central, Eastern, etc.).
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <section id="grading" class="card" aria-labelledby="grading-title">
          <h2 id="grading-title">Grades &amp; Contract-Based Grading</h2>

          <h3>Grade Scale</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>90–100%: A</li>
            <li>80–89%: B</li>
            <li>70–79%: C</li>
            <li>60–69%: D</li>
            <li>0–59%: E</li>
          </ul>

          <details>
            <summary>Important grading notes <span class="hint">(expand)</span></summary>
            <ul>
              <li>Passing grade is A, B, or C (for some programs, D).</li>
              <li>You cannot pass without completing and submitting <strong>all composition assignments</strong> (including drafts).</li>
              <li>Grading turnaround: homework/quizzes within 2 days; composition assignments within 1 week.</li>
              <li>Composition submissions: Word (.doc/.docx) or PDF via Blackboard. Multimodal submissions: MP4. Incorrect formats are not graded.</li>
            </ul>
          </details>

          <h3>Contract Categories</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Specific contract details are available in Blackboard. Contract categories include Workshops &amp; Responses (including lecture videos),
            Quizzes &amp; Conferences, and Composition Assignments (Research Proposal, Annotated Bibliography, Research Paper).
          </p>

          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Contract-based grading expectations table" tabindex="0">
            <table>
              <caption>Contract-Based Grading Expectations</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Contract Grade</th>
                  <th scope="col">Workshops &amp; Responses</th>
                  <th scope="col">Quizzes &amp; Conferences</th>
                  <th scope="col">Composition Assignments</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">A</th>
                  <td>
                    <ul>
                      <li>Peer review fully completed (full draft + every worksheet question for each peer).</li>
                      <li>All workshop activities completed.</li>
                      <li>At least 3 discussion boards completed.</li>
                    </ul>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <ul>
                      <li>Quizzes overall: 85% or better.</li>
                      <li>At least 2 conferences (1 with instructor; other may be writing coach/tutor).</li>
                    </ul>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <ul>
                      <li>Each assignment revised until at least “meets;” at least one must reach “exceeds.”</li>
                      <li>Research Proposal</li>
                      <li>Annotated Bibliography</li>
                      <li>Research Assignment</li>
                    </ul>
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">B</th>
                  <td>
                    <ul>
                      <li>Peer review completed (draft may be incomplete; most worksheet questions answered).</li>
                      <li>At least 75% of workshop activities completed.</li>
                      <li>At least 2 discussion boards completed using the RISE model.</li>
                    </ul>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <ul>
                      <li>Quizzes overall: 70% or better.</li>
                      <li>At least 1 conference with instructor.</li>
                    </ul>
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    <ul>
